# ACTA -- line-ending and binary policy for the PUBLIC repository.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. CI gained a windows-latest runner in 3.0.14, and Git for Windows checks out
# CRLF by default. R's readLines() does NOT strip the trailing \r, and this suite reads its own
# source and compares it: a dozen gates use $-anchored patterns or whole-line matches, so every
# one of them would fail on Windows for a reason that has nothing to do with the code under test.
# A gate failing for the wrong reason is worse than no gate, because the next person switches it
# off. `eol=lf` makes the working tree LF on every platform and removes the whole class.
* text=auto eol=lf

# BINARY, EXPLICITLY. This repository has a documented history of workbook corruption -- see the
# comments in Publish/strip_sharepoint_metadata.py -- so nothing is left to git's content guess.
# A .xlsx is a zip: a single byte "normalised" inside one makes it unreadable, and openxlsx and
# Excel disagree about how loudly to say so.
*.xlsx binary
*.fcs  binary
*.png  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.rds  binary
*.bin  binary

# Windows batch files are the one thing that genuinely wants CRLF: cmd.exe tolerates LF, but a
# label or a line continuation at the end of a LF-only file has bitten people for decades, and
# Setup.bat is the documented Windows entry point.
*.bat text eol=crlf
